# portable_libc: printf's l and ll length modifiers, and unsigned %u/%o/%x.
#
# Applies to: libc64/src/stdio.c (vfprintf, and the helper _int_to_str).
#
# Why: portable_libc's vfprintf skips any 'l'/'L' and then always reads an
# int.  On x86_64 long and long long are 8 bytes, so %ld/%lld/%lu/%llx...
# printed only the low 32 bits, sign-extended (printf("%lld", 1LL << 40)
# printed 0).  It also printed %u/%o/%x through a signed int, so
# printf("%u", 4294967295u) printed -1.  Now: no 'l' reads an int (or
# unsigned int), one 'l' a long, two a long long; %d/%i convert a signed
# value, %u/%o/%x an unsigned one; the digits are produced from an
# unsigned long long magnitude, so LLONG_MIN and ULLONG_MAX print right.
# %i is accepted as %d.  Widths, '0', '+' and '-' behave as before.
# (Under pnut, where long is int, one 'l' reads an int-sized value, which is
# what a pnut-compiled caller passes.)
